    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1260",
      "html": "<p>Africa: known from the Niger River and Volta River system, the White Nile (Barro and Alvero Rivers) and the Omo River; probably also in the Chad and Senegal basins but has not been collected there.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1260",
      "html": "<p>Unreported in the aquarium. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1260",
      "html": "<p>Identification: body with moderately to very large black spots irregularly scattered. Compared to M. electricus, its oral tooth bands are much larger.</p><p>Etymology: Malapterurus comes from the Greek malakos, meaning soft, pteron, meaning fin, and oura, meaning tail; presumably in reference to the adipose fin, which appears all the more prominent in the absence of the rayed dorsal fin.</p>"
    },
